WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.137
Real analytical projects often require information from more than one table.

00:00:05.137 --> 00:00:11.707
In this example, the orders table has customer IDs but does not have the customer segment.

00:00:11.707 --> 00:00:15.667
The customers table contains customer ID and segment.

00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:19.882
We use merge to bring the segment information into the order table.

00:00:19.882 --> 00:00:25.382
The key is customer_id. We use a left join because we want to keep every order,

00:00:25.382 --> 00:00:27.812
even if a customer record is missing.

00:00:27.812 --> 00:00:31.032
Click Run and inspect the merged result.

00:00:31.032 --> 00:00:33.762
The validate argument is very important.

00:00:33.762 --> 00:00:38.412
Many-to-one tells Pandas that many orders may belong to one customer,

00:00:38.412 --> 00:00:42.882
but each customer ID should appear only once in the customer table.

00:00:42.882 --> 00:00:49.502
If that assumption is violated, Pandas can raise an error instead of silently multiplying rows.

00:00:49.502 --> 00:00:54.577
After any merge, check the row count and look for unmatched keys.

00:00:54.577 --> 00:00:59.062
A join can run without errors and still be logically wrong.

00:00:59.062 --> 00:01:03.400
Validation protects us from that kind of silent analytical mistake.
